{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966815,"nonce":"A/A1R0+hGnX8UGG7gwnn3YhmSHU=","title":"Future architectural project — Solid-style Show / For primitives that bring per-child","message":"The v2 reactive-architecture work ([[project-reactive-architecture-v2]]) ships per-property signal binding (`.bg(\u0026signal)`, `.w(\u0026signal)`, etc.) but deliberately scopes out **signal-bound structural reactivity**. Today, any UI that toggles between subtrees or renders a list driven by signal value still requires a `Stateful` rebuild (see [[blinc-elements-not-reactive-stateful-vs-signal-bound]]).\n\nThis memory tracks the architectural project that would close that gap, modelled on Solid's `\u003cShow\u003e` + `\u003cFor\u003e` primitives.\n\n**Why:** Stateful's rebuild path is now the wrong tool for two distinct shapes of work — properties (covered by v2 P2) and structure (this project). Without a structural primitive, every signal that drives \"should this exist\" or \"what entries are in this list\" still pays the closure-rerun + collect + diff + handler-re-registration cost, even when the change is one child swap. Solid's pattern proves the cheap alternative is achievable; the gap is implementation, not concept.\n\n**How to apply:** When asked about closing Stateful's remaining cost or about declarative conditional / list rendering driven by signals, surface this as the relevant future work. Don't propose it as part of any in-flight v2 phase — it's orthogonal to all 9 phases and has its own design surface. Don't promise it as a near-term ship unless prioritised explicitly.\n\n\n1. **`Show` primitive** — signal-bound conditional rendering of a single child:\n   ```rust\n   show(\u0026open_signal).when(|| panel()).fallback(|| closed_handle())\n   ```\n   When `open_signal` changes, the single child slot is swapped (mount new, unmount old). No rebuild of siblings. No closure re-run for descendants whose own props bind to other signals.\n\n2. **`For` primitive** — signal-bound list rendering with key-based reconciliation:\n   ```rust\n   for_each(\u0026items_signal)\n       .key(|item| item.id)\n       .render(|item| row(item))\n   ```\n   When `items_signal` changes (push / remove / reorder), reconciliation diffs by key: surviving entries keep their existing layout nodes + bindings; new entries mint fresh ids; removed entries get `remove_subtree_nodes` cleanup. No closure re-run for unchanged entries.\n\n\nMost of the substrate already exists:\n\n- **StableNodeId** (per [[project-layout-tree-stability-substrate]]) gives identity that survives signal-driven structural change — `For`'s key parameter feeds the same identity derivation pattern.\n- **`remove_subtree_nodes`** already evicts bindings + handlers + animations on node removal — `For`'s unmount path reuses it.\n- **The property channel** (`queue_prop_update_partial` / `queue_layout_update_partial`) handles per-property writes — `Show` / `For` mutations sit at a different layer (child-list mutation) but can share the same drain rhythm.\n- **`PropertyBindingRegistry`** keys on signal id — `Show` / `For` register against their own signal id and fire reconciliation logic instead of property writes.\n\nWhat's NEW:\n- A child-slot abstraction on `Div` that can be replaced atomically (not just appended via `.child`)\n- A reconciler that walks (old_children_by_key, new_children_by_key) and emits mount / unmount / reorder operations\n- A reconciliation channel parallel to the property channel — same queue shape, drain step handles structural ops\n\n\n- `Show` alone: ~2 weeks (child-slot infra + signal subscription + mount/unmount via the existing structural-rebuild path)\n- `For` with key-based reconciliation: ~4-6 weeks (reconciler is the bulk; needs careful interaction with FLIP / motion-bound subtrees / scroll containers that depend on stable child positions)\n- Integration with cn widgets that benefit: another ~1-2 weeks (cn::list, cn::table rows, cn::accordion items, etc.)\n\nTotal: ~7-10 weeks. Bigger than any single v2 phase; smaller than v2 in aggregate.\n\n\nNot blocking any v2 phase. Most cn widget structures are fixed at build time (the children of a `cn::button` don't change based on state — only their styling does), so the CPU wins from v2 P2-2.4 already cover most interactive scenarios. The structural-reactivity gap shows up in:\n- Long lists driven by `State\u003cVec\u003cT\u003e\u003e` (a chat log, a notification stack, a file picker)\n- Modal stacks where each modal mounts / unmounts\n- Conditional UI flows (form steps, wizard pages, route content)\n\nPick this up when any of those becomes the measured hotspot. Until then, `Stateful` is the correct tool for those cases — its rebuild path is real cost but it's the right cost shape for the work it's doing today.\n\n\n- Foundation: [[project-reactive-architecture-v2]] (P2 substrate that this builds on)\n- Today's rule: [[blinc-elements-not-reactive-stateful-vs-signal-bound]] (hybrid pattern + the explicit gap this project closes)\n- Identity layer: [[project-layout-tree-stability-substrate]] (StableNodeId is what makes For's keyed reconciliation tractable)\n- Influence: Solid's `\u003cShow\u003e` / `\u003cFor\u003e` (https://docs.solidjs.com/concepts/control-flow/show + .../for)","files":null}]}